  "content": {
    "id": "p33-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "Consider the graph of $y=x^3-4x^2-3x+18$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p33-q9-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Sketch the graph.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=(x+2)(x-3)^2$. The graph crosses the $x$-axis at $(-2,0)$, touches it at $(3,0)$ and passes through $(0,18)$. Its left end falls and its right end rises.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}x^3-4x^2-3x+18&=(x+2)(x^2-6x+9)\\\\&=(x+2)(x-3)^2.\\end{align*}$$\nThe simple root $x=-2$ gives a crossing; the double root $x=3$ gives a touch. The $y$-intercept is $(0,18)$. The positive cubic leading term gives a falling left end and a rising right end.",
          "solutionDiagrams": [
            {
              "id": "p33-q9-a-solution-graph",
              "role": "solution",
              "format": "tikz",
              "code": "% mathsmap-diagram-colours {\"version\":1,\"kind\":\"graph\",\"base\":[],\"semantic\":[],\"reference\":\"source page 33 question 9(a); teacher page 63\"}\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.85cm,y=0.09cm]\n\\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes=\"1\"/>}\n\\path[use as bounding box] (-3.2,-33) rectangle (5.2,34);\n\\draw[->,black,line width=0.5pt] (-3,-0) -- (5,0) node[right] {$x$};\n\\draw[->,black,line width=0.5pt] (0,-30) -- (0,31) node[above] {$y$};\n\\draw[black,line width=0.8pt,samples=150,domain=-2.8:4.7] plot (\\x,{(\\x+2)*(\\x-3)^2});\n\\node[below left] at (-2,0) {\\special{dvisvgm:raw <g data-graph-text=\"tick\">}$-2$\\special{dvisvgm:raw </g>}};\n\\node[below] at (3,0) {\\special{dvisvgm:raw <g data-graph-text=\"tick\">}$3$\\special{dvisvgm:raw </g>}};\n\\node[right] at (0,18) {\\special{dvisvgm:raw <g data-graph-text=\"tick\">}$18$\\special{dvisvgm:raw </g>}};\n\\end{tikzpicture}",
              "src": null,
              "widthMm": 70,
              "alt": "Mathematical diagram",
              "overlayOf": null,
              "transparent": false,
              "axes": null,
              "spec": {
                "sourcePage": 33,
                "sourceRegion": "Question 9(a); answer graph on teacher PDF page 63, printed page 392",
                "description": "Equation-driven cubic sketch with a crossing at -2, tangency at 3, and y-intercept 18. No grid is supplied in the question. Decorative source curve colour is replaced by black.",
                "mathematics": "y=(x+2)(x-3)^2; simple root -2, double root 3, y-intercept 18; positive leading coefficient.",
                "answerVisibility": "Solution only."
              },
              "derived": false,
              "reviewStatus": "needs-review"
            }
          ]
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      },
      {
        "id": "p33-q9-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "A line through the origin $y=mx$ intersects the graph at $(x_1,y_1)$, $(x_2,y_2)$ and $(x_3,y_3)$. Show that the average $\\dfrac{x_1+x_2+x_3}{3}$ does not depend upon the value of $m$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac{x_1+x_2+x_3}{3}=\\dfrac43$.\n\nVieta’s formula gives $x_1+x_2+x_3=4$, independently of $m$.",
          "worked": "At the intersections,\n$$\\begin{align*}x^3-4x^2-3x+18&=mx\\\\x^3-4x^2-(3+m)x+18&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nBy Vieta’s formula,\n$$\\begin{align*}x_1+x_2+x_3&=-\\frac{-4}{1}\\\\x_1+x_2+x_3&=4\\\\\\frac{x_1+x_2+x_3}{3}&=\\frac43.\\end{align*}$$\nThe average is constant, so it does not depend upon $m$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      }
    ]
  },
